// A named `async fn` is SEALED: its body reaches an outer binding only through `use (…)`, so a
// bare `x = …` naming anything else is a fresh local — a module global of the same name is
// unrelated. The state-machine desugar has to agree with that, because it decides which names get
// hoisted into cells: it excludes the globals a body may *store* to (a bare `g = …` against one is
// a global store, not a local), and it used to take that set as every global regardless of the seal.
//
// The result was a name that read two different values inside one function: the binding wrote a
// local, the reads inside the loop — which live in later machine states, and so resolve through the
// step closure — loaded the global. Before the fix this printed
//
//     before: local-tally | in loop: global-tally | in loop: global-tally
//
// The stakes are higher than one file. A program's globals include everything the linker merged in,
// so a *dependency package's* async fn had its locals decided against the **consuming program's**
// top-level names — para/html's session binds a local `page`, and any app declaring `fn page()`
// (as its own examples do) broke it. A package author cannot see or defend against those names.
// expect: stdout "before: local-tally | in loop: local-tally | in loop: local-tally"
// expect: stdout "global still: global-tally"
// expect: stdout "reachable: reached-through-use"
// expect: exit 0

tally = "global-tally"

mut reachable = "untouched"

async fn tick(n: int): int {
    return n + 1
}

// `tally` is this function's own local, read identically before and inside the loop.
async fn work(): string {
    tally = "local-tally"
    mut out = "before: ${tally}"
    mut n = 0
    while n < 2 {
        out = out ~ " | in loop: ${tally}"
        n = tick(n).await
    }
    return out
}

// The other half of the rule: a global named in `use (…)` IS reachable, and a store to it across a
// suspend still lands on the global — the seal is what decides, not the mere existence of the name.
async fn touch() use (reachable): void {
    n = tick(0).await
    if n == 1 {
        reachable = "reached-through-use"
    }
}

echo work().await
echo "global still: ${tally}"
touch().await
echo "reachable: ${reachable}"
